course: dictionary
lesson: 985
title: Boathouse and the clues in a list
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A boathouse is made of two words joined together: boat and house. When you split a long word into parts you can often guess its meaning. A house for boats is a good first guess, and it is right.
explain: «He tells her about the boathouse, the fish-pond, the walled kitchen-garden, the pig-styes, the stables, the dairy and the banqueting-hall.» Every other item in this list is a building or place on a large estate. So the boathouse is a building, not a boat. Ask yourself: what would a building like this hold?
explain: Look for clues about water too. A story set by a river or lake may mention oars, boats or a rowing club. Those words point to a boathouse, where boats are kept, often right by the water.
fact: A boathouse is a shed or building by a river or lake where boats are kept.
fact: A rowing club's boathouse is where its boats and oars are stored.
fact: Boathouse is a compound word: boat plus house.
fact: Lists in a text can give you clues, because the words in a list are often of the same kind.
fact: In «The Wind in the Willows», Toad Hall has a fine boathouse, and the Water Rat keeps his boat by the river bank.
exercise: «He tells her about the boathouse, the fish-pond, the walled kitchen-garden and the stables.» What is a boathouse? | A kitchen in a walled garden | *A shed or building by a river or lake where boats are kept | A pond full of fish | A stable where horses sleep
exercise: Ravi says a boathouse is a house for people to live in. What do rowing clubs keep in their boathouse? | Bread and cakes | Books and maps | Horses and cows | *Boats and oars
exercise: Boathouse is two smaller words joined together. Which two words make it? | boat and horse | bath and house | boa and thouse | *boat and house
test: «...the dairy and the banqueting-hall.» Where would you expect a boathouse to stand? | Up a mountain | Inside a bank | In a desert | *Near water
test: Which of these is a place where boats are kept? | A stable | A dairy | *A boathouse | A linen-press
exam: Mia reads «the boathouse, the stables and the dairy» and says a boathouse must be a farm building. What is the best clue against her idea? | The word boat means a farm animal | *The boathouse is in a list of buildings, but its name tells you it is for boats, not animals | Mia is right because lists always mean farms | Stables and dairies are both farm buildings, so it is a farm
